# Network Management Application (CS 4398 F26 capstone)

A self-hosted network management system, comparable in purpose to SolarWinds: it monitors
network devices, pushes and versions their configurations, collects their syslog centrally, and
serves DNS and DHCP. Requirements are in
`SRS Requirements/SRS Network Management Application (Capstone).pdf`; the
[docs index](docs/README.md) maps every course deliverable to its file.

## Status

| Track | Features (SRS §4) | State |
|---|---|---|
| A: Platform and monitoring | Device inventory, polling, dashboard, alerts (4.1, 4.2) | Working: encrypted device credentials ([ADR-0008](docs/decisions/0008-encrypted-device-credentials.md)), connection test before adding, SNMP CPU/memory (incl. Cisco MIBs), device-down and CPU-threshold alerts |
| A: Authentication | Login, admin/viewer roles, accounts (4.7) | Working: every API route requires login, viewers are read-only ([ADR-0005](docs/decisions/0005-authentication-and-sessions.md)); idle timeout (R7.5) open |
| B: Configuration and logging | Push, versions, diff, rollback, templates, syslog, search, audit (4.3, 4.4) | Working and validated on the lab (Cisco IOS syslog parsed); version diff computed on the server |
| C: Network services | DNS (4.5), DHCP (4.6) | Working: authoritative DNS zones ([ADR-0006](docs/decisions/0006-authoritative-dns-design.md)); DHCP pools, reservations, leases ([ADR-0007](docs/decisions/0007-dhcp-server-design.md)); Services screen with DNS and DHCP tabs |

Every Essential functional requirement is implemented and tested (408 tests, 95% coverage).
Open: Desirable features (e.g. device groups, DNS forwarding, session idle timeout), HTTPS
(NFR-7), and the performance measurements NFR-1, NFR-2 and NFR-4.

Requirement-level detail: [traceability matrix](docs/verification/traceability-matrix.md).

## Stack

| Layer | Technology |
|---|---|
| API | Python 3.11+, FastAPI, Uvicorn |
| Persistence | SQLAlchemy; SQLite for lab sessions, PostgreSQL in Docker |
| Device configuration | Netmiko over SSH (paramiko pinned below 5 for IOSv, [ADR-0003](docs/decisions/0003-pin-paramiko-for-iosv.md)) |
| Monitoring | icmplib (reachability), PySNMP v2c (CPU, memory, interfaces, throughput; generic and Cisco MIBs) |
| Logging | Built-in UDP/TCP syslog listener on port 514: RFC 5424, Cisco IOS, and RFC 3164 formats |
| Authentication | argon2id password hashes, server-side cookie sessions, one central access policy |
| Secrets | Device credentials encrypted with Fernet (`cryptography`); key from `NETMGR_SECRET_KEY`, never in the database |
| Network services | Authoritative DNS (dnslib) on UDP/TCP 53; DHCP server on UDP 67 |
| Web UI | React + Vite + Recharts in `web/` (talks to the API only) |
| Tests and CI | pytest (coverage gate ≥ 70%), ruff, mypy, `tsc`; GitHub Actions on every PR |
| Virtual lab | Cisco Modeling Labs (CML) with IOSv routers ([lab/README.md](lab/README.md)) |

## Repository layout

```
app/            FastAPI application (models, routes, domain services, device adapters)
  main.py         app lifespan (syslog, poller, DNS, DHCP) and API routes
  auth.py, auth_routes.py, user_routes.py                    login, roles, accounts (Track A)
  credentials.py, connection_test.py                         encrypted device credentials, connection test (Track A)
  monitoring.py, poller.py, probes.py                        monitoring and alerts (Track A)
  config_service.py, configuration.py, config_templates.py, config_diff.py   configuration management (Track B)
  syslog.py       syslog listener, parser, search (Track B)
  dns/, dhcp/     network services (Track C)
tests/          pytest suite; all device I/O is faked (SRS 8.3)
web/            React front end
lab/            CML lab: topology notes, router base configs, setup and smoke test
scripts/        run-lab.ps1 (run natively on Windows), check-lab.ps1 (lab smoke test),
                check_snmp.py (SNMP CPU/memory check), dns_probe.py (DNS query timing)
docs/           design, verification, process, contracts, decision records
```

## Running it

### Natively on Windows, against the CML lab (normal for lab sessions)
CML needs VMware nested virtualization, which stops working while WSL2/Hyper-V (and therefore
Docker Desktop) is on, so lab sessions run the app without Docker:

```powershell
Copy-Item .env.lab.example .env.lab      # then replace every REPLACE_WITH_* value
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\run-lab.ps1
powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts\check-lab.ps1   # in a second terminal
```

`run-lab.ps1` creates `.venv`, installs the project, checks the toolchain, and starts the API
(`http://127.0.0.1:8000`, interactive docs at `/docs`) and the web UI. Full steps, the firewall
rules, and troubleshooting are in [lab/README.md](lab/README.md).

### With Docker (Linux host, or an Ubuntu VM next to CML)
```sh
cp .env.example .env
docker compose up --build
cd web && VITE_API_MODE=live npm run dev
```
Compose runs PostgreSQL and the API, and publishes syslog on UDP/TCP 514.

### Tests and checks
```powershell
python -m venv .venv; .\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
pytest --cov=app        # unit and API tests; no lab or open ports needed
ruff check .
mypy app --ignore-missing-imports
cd web; npm run build   # TypeScript check + production build (Node ^20.19 or >= 22.12)
```

## Devices and credentials

Register devices on the Devices screen (or `POST /api/v1/devices`) with a name, management IP,
SSH port, Netmiko platform (`cisco_ios`), and the SSH username, password and optional enable
secret. **Test and add** first checks that the SSH port answers and the login works; if it fails,
the form says which stage failed and offers **Fix** or **Save anyway** (R1.3).

Credentials are stored encrypted with Fernet. The key comes from `NETMGR_SECRET_KEY` in your
git-ignored `.env.lab` / `.env` and is never stored in the database. The API never returns
credentials, only `has_credentials` ([ADR-0008](docs/decisions/0008-encrypted-device-credentials.md)).
Generate a key once:

```powershell
.venv\Scripts\python -c "from cryptography.fernet import Fernet; print(Fernet.generate_key().decode())"
```

Keep the key safe: if it changes, stored credentials can't be decrypted and must be re-entered.
Devices without stored credentials fall back to `NETMGR_DEVICE_CREDENTIALS_JSON`, keyed by device
name ([ADR-0002](docs/decisions/0002-device-credentials-from-environment.md), lab use).
`CPU_ALERT_PERCENT` (default 90) sets the CPU alert threshold. Never commit credentials or keys.

## Safety

Lab use only. There is no TLS yet (NFR-7), so never expose
this stack to the internet, a campus network, or a production network (SRS 6.2).

## How we work

We develop with a disciplined agentic process, Module of Thought (MoT): explicit agent context
([CLAUDE.md](CLAUDE.md)), a measured baseline, one capability per increment, critical checkpoints
labeled up front, and requirement-named tests that fail before and pass after. See
[docs/process/agentic-workflow.md](docs/process/agentic-workflow.md) and the
[V&V plan](docs/verification/vv-plan.md). Branches follow `track-<a|b|c>/<issue>-<slug>`, and PRs
need green CI and a reviewer from another track.
